{"status":"supported","evidence_ids":["ev-8e63f9e3e870e0f269953422"],"request_dft":false,"proposed_test_ids":[],"rationale":"One admissible record exists: converged_dft, measured, for the exact target structure P-monolayer-ideal-UC (P4). It reports final_max_force = 0.003948 eV/A, below the 0.01 eV/A threshold by about 2.5x. The settings give ediffg = -0.01, so the run used the criterion the claim names, and the VASP marker 'reached required accuracy' agrees with the numeric force. No admissible record disagrees, so the disagreement rule does not apply. Unspecified scope does not widen this narrow claim about one archived run, and the record identifies that run by OUTCAR hash, calculation path and archive commit.

Assumptions: the parsed final_max_force comes from the last ionic step. It is the maximum per-atom force or component, and either definition meets the threshold. The marker is mostly the same criterion restated, so it corroborates the force value and is not an independent measurement. Claude-sonnet's energy-per-atom check is correct arithmetic (-19.77664371/4 = -4.94416093) and shows the record is internally consistent. Gemini-flash's agreement adds no independent evidence, because it rests on the same single record.

The decisive check would be to re-parse the final TOTAL-FORCE block of the archived OUTCAR. The claim is about meeting the stopping criterion, not about physical convergence in cell, k-mesh or vacuum. The tighter-EDIFFG rerun targets non-converged calculations and addresses no visible gap, so I select no follow-up test and no further DFT."}
